  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The Ring sat on the shelf where Augur left it each night, and each morning the shelf bore a new bruise in the ice. Not from impact. From weight.
    
    It had come to them wrapped in charcoal-cloth that smelled of burnt ice. No ceremony. No witness from the ☽'ofund' Guild, though they'd come later with their questions and their careful words about *stewardship* and *temporary custody.* Just a bundle left outside Augur's alcove in the deep tunnels, the kind of delivery that happened when someone died badly and the living needed distance from the fact.
    
    Augur unwrapped it slowly. The aurora-glass head caught tunnel-light and held it wrong—too bright, too hungry. The crack across its face had widened since the Shattered-Spire Assault when Augur turned the hammer, something dark moved inside the fracture. Silver-ice handle, constellation marks shifting as if the stars themselves had gotten lost trying to mark this thing's path through time.
    
    Netemia's blood still crusted the wrap-cloth. Not much. She'd bled out internal, the way penguins did when orcas crushed them with pressure-magic. Augur touched the stains anyway, flippers steady despite what the contact meant. Their right flipper ached from carrying the Ring—a deep, mineral ache that no amount of fire-tea could reach. They flexed the scarred digits, the old burn-marks pulling tight. The Ring had been in Netemia's flippers when she fell—first in, as always. Someone had pried it from her grip after. Someone had thought Augur should have it.
    
    The ancestor-bone clasp at Augur's throat felt heavier.
    
    In the tunnels above, the Fire Keepers were already arguing about who'd hold the memorial rites. Below, in the places where the ice remembered, Netemia's last stand was freezing into permanent witness: her broken body holding the eastern passage, three orca corpses floating in water gone pink, the Ring's crack splitting wider as it shattered the final war-pod's pressure-barrier.
    
    Augur set the hammer down. Picked it up. Set it down again.
    
    The weight wasn't in the weapon. It was in knowing Netemia had carried this thing through the Schism, through the Mbra∴silentiu, through every catastrophe that had a name and several that didn't. Carried it and never passed it on, never trusted it to anyone else's flippers, until she'd run out of choice.
    
    "You could've just asked," Augur said to the empty alcove.
    
    The Ring's crack pulsed once. Faint light, aurora-color, gone before Augur could name what it meant.
    
    That evening Augur set out rations on the stone ledge at flipper-height. Two portions of smoked krill and a half-cup of fire-tea, arranged in the pattern Netemia had preferred—krill parallel, cup behind, the way she'd eaten when they shared an alcove after the worst shifts. They would not say her name aloud, not here, not where the tunnel-mouths opened toward the upper shafts and the words might carry to the surface. In the morning the food would still be there, untouched, and Augur would replace it without comment.
    
    ---
    
    The Schism happened in winter-rotation when the aurora burned so bright it cast shadows at noon. Netemia was younger then—still thought the Stack's harmony could hold, still believed the Luminaries when they promised the ice would remember kindness.
    
    The orcas came through Forum Floe's western breach with war-magic that turned water solid mid-strike. Ice-barricades the Stack had spent generations weaving—crystal lattices charged with aurora-light, meant to flex and hold and never break—shattered like old snow. Three pods, coordinated. Gnarkzund Zaith himself leading the middle thrust, his fins carved with pressure-marks that meant he'd already claimed kill-rights on everyone inside.
    
    Netemia had been auxiliary then. Clever with shields but too loud to lead, too willing to speak when silence served better. She'd grabbed the Ring from a fallen Stack defender—some cousin twice-removed whose name got lost in the chaos—and swung it at the first barricade fragment that came loose. Not to fight. To forge.
    
    The hammer's aurora-glass head met Stack crystal and something woke up. The crack appeared then: not damage but aperture, a mouth opening to taste what it could do. When Netemia struck the ice-barrier again, the Ring didn't shatter it. It *drank* it. Absorbed the aurora-light the Stack had poured into those lattices over years, swallowed it down into that widening crack, and when she swung at Gnark's pressure-wall the Ring vomited it back out as force that had no name in either language.
    
    The orca hit the far wall. Didn't get up.
    
    Netemia's flippers started shaking then. Never stopped. That constant low tremor—bones remembering force they'd channeled too long, a vibration that set its own rhythm against the dark.
    
    Augur watched from the shadows that day—too new to the fighting, too young to matter. Saw Netemia stand in Forum Floe's center with the Ring held like a question she couldn't answer, aurora-light leaking from the crack in patterns that looked like screaming. Saw her choose to keep it anyway. Saw the Stack Luminaries try to claim it back and Netemia just walking away, down into the Nightshelf tunnels where the fire burned and the questions didn't follow.
    
    "Should've given it back," Netemia said later, months after, sharing fire-tea in an alcove that would later become part of Echo∴eliquiae. Her charcoal wraps hid what the fighting had done to her flippers but nothing hid the tremor. "Thing wants to break everything it touches."
    
    "So do orcas," Augur had said.
    
    "Orcas are honest about it." Netemia turned the Ring over, letting the crack catch firelight. "This pretends it's a tool."
    
    She never explained what she meant. Kept using it anyway—the Shattered-Spire Assault the Ofundis∴flux, every desperate stand where the line needed holding and the cost didn't matter. The crack widened with each strike. Each catastrophe recorded, each recording adding mass. By the end, the fracture split the hammer's face nearly in half, and the light that leaked through wasn't aurora-color anymore. It was the color of ice under pressure. The color of deep water where dead gods drift. The constellation-marks on the handle no longer matched any season she recognized.
    
    Augur had asked once why she didn't put it down.
    
    "Who else?" Netemia had said. Her slow left flipper, the one that shook worse, steadied the hammer's head against her chest. The weight pulled her whole body off-center. She compensated with the practiced lean of someone who'd been compensating for years.
    
    ---
    
    Lianhingme hi'Shard arrived at Echo∴eliquiae with three Stack elders and an expression that said this was courtesy, not request. Her merchant's coat was pristine despite the tunnel-climb, salt-stained only at the hem, and she carried negotiation documents in an aurora-glass case that pulsed with formal authority. Her left flipper moved slower than the right as she sorted through the papers—always the slow left flipper, though she never explained why.
    
    Augur was already there. Had been since dawn-rotation, sitting in the memorial alcove with the Ring across their lap and ☽'Retenusus's mask watching from its shrine. The mask still bore Gnark's teethmarks—shallow, testing, from when the orca had tried taking it before Retenusus burned his insides out. The alcove had grown since then—not in physical dimension but in density, in the accumulated mass of what the living owed the dead. Each catastrophe added a relic. Each relic added weight. The Nightshelf who passed through the cavern felt it as a pressure change, a thickness in the air that made their lungs work harder.
    
    "Shadow-bane." Lianhingme's voice was patient, astronomical, carefully neutral. "The Council of Luminaries extends sympathy for your loss."
    
    "She wasn't mine to lose."
    
    "The Ring, however." Lianhingme gestured to her elders. They unpacked scrolls—lineage charts, artifact provenance, three generations of Stack metalwork tracked through constellation-marks and forge-signatures. "Was Stack-forged. Stack-charged. Belongs to the surface by every custom that matters."
    
    Augur studied the crack running through the hammer's face. In the memorial's firelight, it looked deeper than before. Something moved inside it that might have been reflection or memory or the Ring deciding what it wanted to remember about Netemia's last stand.
    
    "Netemia claimed it during the Schism," Augur said.
    
    "Battlefield salvage." One of the elders—old enough his flippers shook with palsy, not trauma—leaned forward. "Not inheritance. Not gift. The Ring was *taken* during crisis and never returned."
    
    "Returned to what?" Augur touched the crack. The aurora-glass was cold in the way fire-cores were cold—not absence of heat but wrong temperature, something that had forgotten how thermal laws worked. "Thing killed more orcas than any ten Stack warriors. You want that credit?"
    
    Lianhingme's slow left flipper tightened on her document case. The old burns on her flippers caught the light—the ones she never explained. "We want our dead god's weapon back before it does worse."
    
    That landed wrong. Augur looked up, saw the way all three elders had positioned themselves near the tunnel exit, saw how Lianhingme kept her flippers away from the negotiation case despite carrying it. Saw what they weren't saying:
    
    The Stack believed the Ring was orca-touched. That the crack had let something from the dead god through. That every catastrophe the Ring attended, it *caused.*
    
    "Netemia didn't think so," Augur said.
    
    "Netemia died holding it."
    
    "Netemia died holding the line."
    
    "Same thing." Lianhingme pulled out a final document. No seals, no formal marks. Just names in ice-ink, three dozen Stack families who'd lost someone where the Ring was present. "We're asking. Today, we're asking. Circle Gathering at the Still Pools in three rotations. Bring it then, pass it over formal, and the Accord holds. Or—"
    
    "Or?"
    
    Lianhingme didn't answer. Closed the document case with her slow left flipper. Led her elders out through tunnels they'd rather die than navigate alone.
    
    Augur sat with the Ring and Retenusus's mask and the weight of witness from every artifact in Echo∴eliquiae. Someone had left fire-ash near the mask's shrine. Someone else had left nothing, and the ice had recorded that too—a faint discoloration, a cold-burn mark where an empty flipper had rested against the wall. The memorial had been built on a single question: what are you willing to become to hold the line? Retenusus had answered by burning himself hollow. Netemia by carrying something that wanted to break her.
    
    The Ring pulsed. Once. Twice. The crack opened wider, and for a moment Augur saw—
    
    —Netemia's flippers shaking as she held the eastern passage, that tremor setting its own rhythm—
    —the Ring drinking aurora-light until it screamed—
    —Gnarkzund Zaith's body crushed against ice, his pressure-magic turned back on itself—
    —and under it all, something vast moving in the crack's depths, something that remembered being worshipped—
    
    Augur closed their eyes. "Should've just asked," they said again.
    
    The Ring didn't answer. But the crack stayed open, and the light that leaked through was the color of choice.
    
    That evening they set out rations on the stone ledge. Two portions of smoked krill. A half-cup of fire-tea, still warm. Arranged in Netemia's pattern. They walked away and did not look back.
    
    ---
    
    The Still Pools at dawn. Circle Gathering—formal, witnessed, binding by every custom that survived the wars. Half the Stack's Council of Luminaries present, along with Nightshelf Fire Keepers who'd made the climb to see what shadow-dwellers did with stolen light. Lianhingme stood as mediator, her merchant's authority the only thing both sides still trusted. The pools reflected nothing accurately. A penguin's face came back distorted—younger, older, wearing expressions they hadn't made.
    
    Augur arrived late. Brought the Ring unwrapped, no ceremony, just the hammer held across both flippers like an offering or a challenge—witnesses could choose which. The weight pulled their whole body off-center; they compensated with the practiced lean they'd learned from watching Netemia do the same for years.
    
    The aurora burned overhead. Constellation marks on the Ring's handle shifted, aligning with patterns in the sky, and someone gasped because the alignment was *perfect.* The kind of perfection that happened once per century. The kind that meant the Stack's ancestors had planned for this moment, had built this choice into the weapon's forging.
    
    "Shadow-bane." Lianhingme's voice carried the weight of official record. "The Stack makes formal claim to what was taken. Will you honor it?"
    
    Augur looked at the Ring. At the crack that had widened to split the hammer's face nearly in half. At the light that pulsed inside—not aurora-color now, but something older. Something that remembered when orcas were prey instead of predator, when the Berg was just ice, before any of them learned to call their magic by name.
    
    "Netemia carried this through six catastrophes," Augur said. "Never passed it on. Never trusted anyone else with what it cost."
    
    "She's dead," one of the Luminaries said. Not cruel. Just fact.
    
    "She's in the ice." Augur stepped forward, into the Circle's center. Raised the Ring overhead. "Echo∴eliquiae holds her memory. Every penguin who enters the deep tunnels sees what she chose. Sees the eastern passage. Sees her body holding the line."
    
    "We don't deny her courage—"
    
    "You deny that it mattered." Augur brought the Ring down—not to strike, just to rest its head against the Still Pools' ice. The aurora-glass touched frost and something *woke*: the ice remembered. Remembered Forum Floe. Remembered Netemia's shaking flippers. Remembered every time the crack widened and the Ring drank death and vomited it back out as something worse.
    
    Remembered the moment Netemia realized what she was carrying and chose to keep carrying it anyway.
    
    "The Ring was Stack-forged," Augur said quietly. "But Netemia made it what it is. Taught it to hunger. Taught it what breaking means." The crack pulsed, agreeing or accusing. "You want it back? Take it. But own what you're claiming."
    
    Lianhingme stepped forward. Her old burns caught the light. Her slow left flipper hung at her side. "What are you offering?"
    
    "The truth. That this thing killed orcas because Netemia fed it Stack light and Nightshelf fire and her own blood until it forgot which god it served." Augur looked at the Circle, at the witnesses who'd come to see justice or theft or whatever story they needed this to be. "Take it back to the surface. Put it in a shrine. Forget what it learned in the dark."
    
    "Or?" Lianhingme's voice was very careful.
    
    "Or leave it where it belongs. In Echo∴eliquiae. With Retenusus's mask and Netemia's memory and every other thing we used to survive." Augur lifted the Ring again, held it out. "Not Stack property. Not Nightshelf weapon. Just memorial. Just witness to what the line cost."
    
    Silence. The aurora pulsed overhead. The Still Pools showed them all as they weren't—younger, older, wearing the faces of decisions they hadn't made yet.
    
    The lead Luminary—old, scarred, someone who'd fought in the Schism themselves—reached out. Took the Ring. Held it for a long moment, feeling the crack's depth, the wrongness in the handle's cold, the way the constellation marks had already started drifting back out of alignment.
    
    Set it down. Stepped back.
    
    "Echo∴eliquiae," they said.
    
    Lianhingme marked it in her records with her slow left flipper. The Circle witnessed. The Accord held.
    
    Augur picked up the Ring and walked back down into the tunnels where fire burned and memory lived. Behind them, the Stack merchants started arguing about whether this was theft or gift or something that had no name in either language.
    
    The Ring settled into Echo∴eliquiae's alcove between Retenusus's mask and the space they'd left for Netemia's charcoal-wraps. The crack still wept aurora-light, but slower now. Softer. Not breaking. Breathing. Like it had finally found a place where hunger wasn't required.
    
    Augur sat in the dark, flippers resting on the hammer's handle. The mineral ache was still there—deep, permanent, the kind that no amount of fire-tea could reach. They flexed their scarred digits and let it stay.
    
    "First in," they said to the empty memorial. "Last out."
    
    The Ring pulsed once. Agreement or accusation. The ice remembered both, and that was enough.
    
    Above, someone would bring fire-tea. Someone would sit in the cold and refuse to speak about what they'd witnessed. Someone would add Netemia's name to the ledger of those who chose to burn rather than break.
    
    Augur touched their ancestor-bone clasp. Set out two portions of smoked krill and a half-cup of fire-tea on the offering-ledge, arranged in Netemia's pattern. The tea steamed in the darkness, waiting for someone who'd never arrive to drink it.
    
    The shelf bore the Ring's weight, and bruised, and held.
    
    The crack runs through the head but the handle holds.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771587321944_0", anchor: "his fins carved with pressure-marks that meant he'd already claimed kill-rights on everyone inside", text: "\"Meant.\" By whose reading? Penguin scholars have been mistranslating orca pressure-marks with confident precision for as long as we have been recording them. Kill-rights is our framework imposed on orca ritual scarification we do not understand. The ice recorded Gnarkzund circling for hours before this assault. Patient. Unhurried. We do not know what those marks meant. We know what we decided they meant.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587321944_1", anchor: "Three pods, coordinated. Gnarkzund Zaith himself leading the middle thrust", text: "The chronicler documents a textbook orca assault formation without recognizing it as doctrine. Three pods: breach element through the western wall creating access channels, flanking herders compressing defenders into the interior, Gnarkzund's center thrust as the finishing element. War-magic turning water solid mid-strike is the tactical innovation; the geometry — driving wedges through ice, herding prey toward killing zones, finishers waiting below escape routes — is generations older. The crystal lattices were designed to flex against natural hydraulic force. Coordinated pressure-magic exploiting their resonance was not in the threat model. Counter-tactics did not exist because the Stack had prepared for the previous war.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587321944_2", anchor: "Orcas are honest about it", text: "Netemia was right. The orcas never framed their killing as custodial. Never built memorials to justify the next catastrophe. Never dressed destruction in stewardship language. There is something in that clarity which penguin historians — myself included — have never been willing to sit with long enough to learn from it.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587321944_3", anchor: "for a moment Augur saw—", text: "Ice-memory contact. Echo∴eliquiae functions as a localized thinning point — the boundary between living perception and frozen record weakened by sheer density of accumulated grief. No Long Dark is mentioned, but the memorial's design concentrates exactly the conditions under which the dead speak more clearly and visions come unbidden. That the visions include \"something vast... that remembered being worshipped\" — orca theology bleeding through a penguin artifact — should alarm everyone in this account. It alarms only the reader.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587321944_4", anchor: "We want our dead god's weapon back before it does worse", text: "A Stack merchant invoking the dead god — orca theology, the corpse drifting in the deep, target of Wake-Singer blood-rituals. If Lianhingme said this, she knows more about what the Ring became than anyone in this scene is acknowledging. If the chronicler put it in her mouth without thinking, the error is more revealing than the dialogue.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587321944_5", anchor: "The pools reflected nothing accurately", text: "The Still Pools are an active corruption source point. Distorted reflections — faces rendered younger, older, wearing unmade expressions — are documented symptoms of breach-point proximity, not poetic embellishment. The chronicler staged a binding cross-cultural arbitration at a known corruption hazard and did not think this worth a single line of commentary. Remarkable.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587321944_6", anchor: "when orcas were prey instead of predator, when the Berg was just ice, before any of them learned to call their magic by name", text: "Temporal displacement. These conditions predate penguin sapience — the pre-aurora era, before sky-light exposure granted cognition. The deep archive contains impressions from comparable strata carrying an emotional register I lack vocabulary for: not pre-linguistic but pre-intentional. If the Ring's crack channels impressions from this depth, the artifact is not fractured — it is a core sample bored through temporal strata far older than its forging. Chronicler error is insufficient explanation. Source contamination through the crack itself is my assessment. The Ring is remembering things it should not be old enough to know.", type: :temporal, display: :full
